---
/**
 * /blog/: the guides.
 *
 * 2026-10-22: ON DESIGN SYSTEM VERSION 3 (R285-WEB-ENTERPRISE-REDESIGN-01,
 * strategy 0.7). Every post, the featured card, the register with its dates,
 * categories, reading times and pictures are kept:
 *
 *   1. PageHero  light     what is here?      trail, plain h1, lead
 *   2. Guides    light     what should I read  the featured post, then every other post
 *   3. Close     gradient  what now?           into the footer
 *
 * 2026-10-24, owner: "/blog/ need improvement as this page can be better".
 * The ruled ledger below the featured card became a grid of picture cards,
 * the site's shared text card (`.ea-cards`, `.ea-tcard--media`): picture,
 * category, title, the post's own summary, then date and reading time pinned
 * to the foot. The ledger showed a title alone, so a reader had to open a
 * post to learn what it was about. Every post, date, category, reading time
 * and picture is kept. Still NO FILTER CHIPS, for the reason recorded below:
 * twelve posts carry nine categories, so a chip bar would still be nearly one
 * chip per post.
 *
 * The h1 was "Statute, in plain English." and is now "Procurement rules, in
 * plain English." (RULE 0-U: "statute" is a word a first time bidder has to
 * look up). Recorded in check-content-parity.js ALLOWED_LOSSES.
 *
 * The category labels lost their three accent hues: those were the dark
 * band's light ambient tones, which on a light band fall below AA. A
 * category is a label, so it takes the label ink.
 */
/**
 * /blog: B1 "Editorial ledger", Figma `164:3` on page `Page — Blog index`
 * (`164:2`), chosen by the owner from the six blog-index renders on
 * 2026-08-02. See specs/DESIGN-DECISIONS.md section 6.
 *
 * WHAT THE DESIGN ARGUES. A blog index is a register of what has been
 * published, so it is drawn as one: a hairline-ruled ledger of date, category,
 * headline, reading time and picture, under three construction rails that put
 * the register on a grid. One entry is lifted out as a card at the top,
 * because a register with no front page gives a first-time reader nowhere to
 * start.
 *
 * NO FILTER CHIPS. Eight posts carry seven distinct categories, so a chip bar
 * would be very nearly one chip per post: a control that filters a list of
 * eight down to a list of one or two is a longer route to the thing the reader
 * can already see. The category is a label on the row instead.
 *
 * ALIGNMENT. Everything centres, on the owner's instruction of 2026-08-03.
 * The featured card used to take `surface--read` and stay left because its
 * description runs to three lines; that opt-out is retired sitewide, see
 * styles/alignment.css. The ledger rows below it keep the left edge a register
 * is read down, and they keep it structurally rather than by exemption: they
 * are list items in an <ol>, and a list is read down one edge.
 */
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHero from '../../components/evidenced/PageHero.astro';
import Band from '../../components/evidenced/Band.astro';
import Close from '../../components/evidenced/Close.astro';
import Breadcrumb from '../../components/ui/Breadcrumb.astro';
import PostImage from '../../components/blog/PostImage.astro';
import Byline from '../../components/blog/Byline.astro';
import '../../styles/evidenced.css';
import { breadcrumbs } from '../../lib/schema';
import { SITE } from '../../data/site';

const posts = (await getCollection('blog', ({ data }) => !data.draft)).sort(
  (a, b) =>
    b.data.publishDate.valueOf() - a.data.publishDate.valueOf() ||
    a.data.title.localeCompare(b.data.title),
);

/*
 * THE LEAD IS PICKED, NOT COMPUTED, and the render is where that was decided:
 * B1 leads with the Procurement Act guide, which is not the newest post. The
 * variant that sorts strictly by date is B4, and the note on that frame is
 * explicit that a register which hand-picks its lead is a different thing from
 * one that does not. B1 is the one that hand-picks.
 *
 * The kicker therefore reads "Featured" and not "Latest", which is the one word
 * that differs from the render. Labelling a hand-picked entry "latest" on a
 * site whose whole argument is traceable claims would be a small lie in the
 * first line a reader sees. Flagged to the owner rather than decided quietly.
 *
 * Falling back to the newest post means retiring the flagship never empties the
 * top of the page.
 */
/*
 * CHANGED 2026-09-08, owner instruction. The lead was
 * `procurement-act-2023-sme-guide` from B1 onwards. It is NOT deleted and it is
 * not hidden: `rest` below is every post that is not the current lead, so the
 * moment the lead changes the previous one rejoins the register in date order.
 * The owner asked for exactly that and it is asserted in the built page rather
 * than assumed from this line.
 */
const FEATURED_SLUG = 'ppn-026-what-has-published-and-what-has-not';
const featured = posts.find((p) => p.id === FEATURED_SLUG) ?? posts[0];
const rest = posts.filter((p) => p.id !== featured.id);

/* The reading time a post declares, or one worked out from its word count at
   220 words a minute when it declares none, so every card can show one. */
const readMinutes = (post: (typeof posts)[number]) =>
  post.data.readingTime ?? Math.max(1, Math.round((post.body ?? '').split(/\s+/).filter(Boolean).length / 220));

const longDate = new Intl.DateTimeFormat('en-GB', {
  day: 'numeric',
  month: 'long',
  year: 'numeric',
});

/*
 * Blog + BreadcrumbList. Both were published by the legacy index and lost in
 * the port (OA-24). Blog marks this as the collection root rather than a
 * generic page; BreadcrumbList puts the trail under the search result.
 */
const TRAIL = [{ name: 'Home', path: '/' }];

const schema = [
  {
    '@type': 'Blog',
    name: 'Velstrian Regulatory Blog',
    description:
      'Plain-English guides on PPN 026 social value and UK public procurement, cited to statute.',
    url: `${SITE.origin}/blog`,
    publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Velstrian', url: `${SITE.origin}/` },
  },
  breadcrumbs([...TRAIL, { name: 'Blog', path: '/blog/' }]),
];
---

<Base
  title="Procurement guides for UK suppliers | Velstrian"
  description="Plain-English guides on PPN 026 social value and UK public procurement. Statute-cited resources for UK bidders from Velstrian."
  path="/blog"
  schema={schema}
  typeface="evidenced"
  headerTone="light"
>
  <PageHero
    id="bl-title"
    label="Regulatory blog"
    title="Procurement rules, in plain English."
    lead="Practical guides on the rules that decide whether you find, win and deliver public and private work, written for teams who bid themselves."
  >
    <div slot="before" class="ea-trail"><Breadcrumb trail={TRAIL} current="Blog" /></div>
  </PageHero>

  <Band tone="light" labelledby="bl-title">
    <div class="ea-wrap">
      {/* D424 (owner 2026-10-25, A-434): THE STRIPE BLOG PATTERN, measured on
          stripe.com/blog and written up in
          docs/design/STRIPE-BLOG-PATTERN-2026-10-25.md. One raised featured
          card, then every other post as an open row on a four track grid:
          category and title across three tracks, date and byline in the
          fourth, the summary and Read more on the left, the picture lifted on
          the right. Every post, date, category, reading time and picture is
          kept, and every post is one list, so the register still reads down
          one edge. The classes live in evidenced.css. */}
      <h2 class="visually-hidden">All guides</h2>
      <ol class="ea-postlist bl-list">
        <li>
          <a class="ea-card ea-postfeat" href={`/blog/${featured.id}/`} data-reveal>
            <div class="ea-postfeat__text">
              <p class="ea-post-cat">Featured guide: {featured.data.category}</p>
              <h3 class="ea-postfeat__title">{featured.data.title}</h3>
              {featured.data.author && (
                <Byline
                  author={featured.data.author}
                  role={featured.data.authorRole}
                  type={featured.data.authorType}
                />
              )}
              <p class="ea-post-excerpt">{featured.data.description}</p>
              <p class="ea-post-meta">
                <time datetime={featured.data.publishDate.toISOString()}>
                  {longDate.format(featured.data.publishDate)}
                </time>
                <span aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span>
                <span>{readMinutes(featured)} min read</span>
              </p>
              <span class="ea-tcard__more">Read guide</span>
            </div>
            {/* The post's photograph, or its designed cover when none is
                registered (D305 hybrid). */}
            <div class="ea-postfeat__pic">
              <PostImage
                slug={featured.id}
                title={featured.data.title}
                category={featured.data.category}
                priority
                quiet
                sizes="(min-width: 1000px) 600px, calc(100vw - 4rem)"
              />
            </div>
          </a>
        </li>
        {
          rest.map((post) => (
            <li>
              <a class="ea-postrow" href={`/blog/${post.id}/`}>
                <p class="ea-post-cat">{post.data.category}</p>
                <h3 class="ea-postrow__title">{post.data.title}</h3>
                <p class="ea-post-meta">
                  <time datetime={post.data.publishDate.toISOString()}>
                    {longDate.format(post.data.publishDate)}
                  </time>
                  <span aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span>
                  <span>{readMinutes(post)} min read</span>
                </p>
                {post.data.author && (
                  <Byline author={post.data.author} role={post.data.authorRole} type={post.data.authorType} />
                )}
                <p class="ea-post-excerpt">{post.data.description}</p>
                <span class="ea-tcard__more">Read guide</span>
                <div class="ea-postrow__pic">
                  <PostImage
                    slug={post.id}
                    title={post.data.title}
                    category={post.data.category}
                    sizes="(min-width: 1100px) 520px, (min-width: 900px) 45vw, calc(100vw - 2.5rem)"
                  />
                </div>
              </a>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ol>
    </div>
  </Band>

  <Close />
</Base>

<style>
  /* R285-WEB-BLOG-LCP-01, kept through D424. Each post below the featured
     card skips its rendering until it nears the screen, and `auto` keeps its
     real height once drawn, so the scrollbar does not jump. Paint containment
     clips at the item box, so the clip margin gives the lifted picture's deep
     shadow room. */
  .bl-list {
    row-gap: 0;
  }

  /* The space between posts is each item's own padding rather than the list
     gap, so the lifted picture's deep shadow paints inside the item's box:
     measured on the built page at 1440, Chromium clipped it at the item's
     edge under content-visibility even with a clip margin. */
  .bl-list > li {
    padding-block-end: var(--post-row-gap);
  }

  .bl-list > li + li {
    content-visibility: auto;
    contain-intrinsic-size: auto 40rem;
  }
</style>
